1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,424
Signal-to-noise ratio is the answer to one of radio's most maddening puzzles: why

2
00:00:07,424 --> 00:00:09,634
turning up the gain doesn't help.

3
00:00:09,634 --> 00:00:10,773
Here's the scene.

4
00:00:10,773 --> 00:00:14,322
A distant system won't decode, so you raise the gain.

5
00:00:14,322 --> 00:00:19,077
The signal climbs on the meter — taller, brighter — and still: nothing.

6
00:00:19,077 --> 00:00:22,492
Because the noise floor climbed by exactly as much.

7
00:00:22,492 --> 00:00:25,907
The receiver never cared how strong the signal was.

8
00:00:25,907 --> 00:00:28,519
It cares about one thing only: the gap.

9
00:00:28,969 --> 00:00:34,015
That gap is the signal-to-noise ratio: the difference, in decibels, between the

10
00:00:34,015 --> 00:00:36,251
signal's power and the noise floor.

11
00:00:36,251 --> 00:00:41,489
A signal at minus eighty-five dBm over a minus one-oh-five dBm floor has twenty dB

12
00:00:41,489 --> 00:00:41,936
of SNR.

13
00:00:41,936 --> 00:00:47,046
It is the single best predictor of whether a signal will decode — enough SNR and

14
00:00:47,046 --> 00:00:51,709
the bits come out clean; too little and errors overwhelm the demodulator.

15
00:00:51,709 --> 00:00:56,883
And because it's a difference of two readings, any constant calibration offset in

16
00:00:56,883 --> 00:01:01,674
the receiver cancels out — which is why SNR is meaningful even on cheap SDR

17
00:01:01,674 --> 00:01:04,676
hardware that can't report true absolute power.

18
00:01:05,126 --> 00:01:09,229
One honest footnote before the payoff: SNR depends on bandwidth.

19
00:01:09,229 --> 00:01:14,102
Noise power scales with the width you integrate it over — about three dB per

20
00:01:14,102 --> 00:01:19,231
doubling — so an SNR figure is only well defined against a stated bandwidth, and

21
00:01:19,231 --> 00:01:23,014
comparing across systems means normalising to a common one.

22
00:01:23,014 --> 00:01:27,822
The practical upside: narrowing the receive filter to the signal's occupied

23
00:01:27,822 --> 00:01:29,874
bandwidth improves SNR directly.

24
00:01:29,874 --> 00:01:33,080
It admits less noise while keeping all the signal.

25
00:01:33,530 --> 00:01:38,191
Now the concept that makes digital radio feel binary: the threshold.

26
00:01:38,191 --> 00:01:43,813
Every digital mode has a threshold SNR below which the error rate climbs steeply —

27
00:01:43,813 --> 00:01:48,611
the curve is flat above it, then collapses within just a few dB below.

28
00:01:48,611 --> 00:01:52,519
Analog FM voice becomes readable around ten to twelve dB.

29
00:01:52,519 --> 00:01:57,661
C4FM and CQPSK digital modes like P25 want roughly fifteen to twenty at the

30
00:01:57,661 --> 00:02:03,008
demodulator for a clean lock, with forward error correction buying some margin

31
00:02:03,008 --> 00:02:03,762
below that.

32
00:02:03,762 --> 00:02:06,915
Three dB over threshold is usable but fragile.

33
00:02:06,915 --> 00:02:08,561
Ten over is comfortable.

34
00:02:08,561 --> 00:02:12,811
That cliff is why digital signals seem either perfect or gone.

35
00:02:13,261 --> 00:02:14,674
Let's make that real.

36
00:02:14,674 --> 00:02:20,189
GopherTrunk reports per-channel demodulator SNR and EVM — error vector magnitude —

37
00:02:20,189 --> 00:02:23,485
so watch two replays of the same control channel.

38
00:02:23,485 --> 00:02:28,126
First capture: the SNR readout sits in the high teens — right in that

39
00:02:28,126 --> 00:02:33,709
fifteen-to-twenty clean-lock zone — EVM is low, sync locks, and the decode counters

40
00:02:33,709 --> 00:02:34,381
just tick.

41
00:02:34,381 --> 00:02:39,157
Second capture, same channel, weaker: the readout hovers around ten dB.

42
00:02:39,157 --> 00:02:44,269
The signal is plainly there on the waterfall — and the decoder gets nothing.

43
00:02:44,269 --> 00:02:45,681
Nothing else changed.

44
00:02:45,681 --> 00:02:50,793
Same software, same channel — five or six dB of gap is the entire difference

45
00:02:50,793 --> 00:02:53,618
between a working receiver and a dead one.

46
00:02:53,618 --> 00:02:58,932
That readout also tells you what kind of problem you have: a failed decode with

47
00:02:58,932 --> 00:03:04,246
healthy SNR isn't a weak signal — go look for overload or wrong tuning instead.

48
00:03:04,696 --> 00:03:07,592
So how do you win dB you can actually keep?

49
00:03:07,592 --> 00:03:09,613
Not the gain slider — the gap.

50
00:03:09,613 --> 00:03:10,758
A better antenna.

51
00:03:10,758 --> 00:03:12,442
A higher or clearer site.

52
00:03:12,442 --> 00:03:16,686
Correct gain staging, a low-noise amplifier, matched filtering.

53
00:03:16,686 --> 00:03:20,929
That's what moves a marginal signal from un-decodable to clean.

54
00:03:20,929 --> 00:03:26,520
And there's a ceiling on what SNR can ever buy: channel capacity grows with the log

55
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:32,111
of one plus SNR, so every doubling of SNR yields diminishing returns — which is why

56
00:03:32,111 --> 00:03:36,826
real systems just aim to sit a few dB above their threshold, reliably.

57
00:03:37,276 --> 00:03:42,596
So: signal-to-noise ratio is the gap, not the height — every mode lives or dies at

58
00:03:42,596 --> 00:03:47,722
its threshold, and the way past a marginal signal is a better gap, not a bigger

59
00:03:47,722 --> 00:03:49,409
number on the gain slider.

60
00:03:49,409 --> 00:03:51,160
Full write-up linked below.

